lass=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"SS3orAfVUf"},{"type":"text","value":" , and that the null value is 0.","position":{"start":{"line":49,"column":1},"end":{"line":49,"column":1}},"key":"xj5QqiVXYb"}],"key":"uKgSgn724Z"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"Z9LuINM7qw"}],"key":"n2w7Ub5yGz"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"Recall that the purpose of this survey was to examine whether the opinions of females and males *differ*with respect to the importance of looks vs. personality. The hypotheses in this case are therefore:\n\n```{figure} images/image030.gif\n:alt: H_0: μ_1 - μ_2 = 0, H_a: μ_1 - μ_2 ≠ 0\n```\n\nwhere μ~1~ represents the mean importance for females and μ~2~ represents the mean importance for males.\n\nIt is important to understand that conceptually, the two hypotheses claim:\n\nH~o~: Score (of looks vs. personality) is not related to gender\n\nH~a~: Score (of looks vs. personality) is related to gender","position":{"start":{"line":52,"column":1},"end":{"line":64,"column":1}},"key":"sT91wnEmcL"}],"key":"AuCJuTfXJm"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":67,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Did I Get This?","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":67,"column":1}},"key":"oet92ZZHW7"}],"identifier":"did-i-get-this","label":"Did I Get This?","html_id":"did-i-get-this","implicit":true,"key":"s0RWxKt09j"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":69,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In order to check the claim that the pregnancy length of women who smoke during pregnancy is shorter, on average, than the pregnancy length of women who do not smoke, a random sample of 35 pregnant women who smoke and a random sample of 35 pregnant women who do not smoke were chosen and their pregnancy lengths were recorded. Here is a figure of this example:","position":{"start":{"line":69,"column":1},"end":{"line":69,"column":1}},"key":"xk9LGEkDko"}],"key":"MAUeW8ymub"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/textbook/build/image156-172cbdbe658635e4d39b08fed53ba2af.gif","alt":"The Smoking (X) variable gives us our two populations. These are Population 1: Pregnant women who smoke, and Pop 2: Pregnant Women who don't smoke. For each of these populations we have the variable Length (Y) and its mean. For smokers we have μ_1, and for non-smokers we have μ_2. From the population of smokers, we create an SRS of size 35, and from the population of non-smokers we create an SRS of 35.","key":"ktZBevHVih","urlSource":"images/image156.gif"}],"enumerator":"2","key":"WecwIQhqLQ"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note","key":"exW8NrMGVQ"}],"key":"F17roST1wW"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"**Sectionnest**\n\n*(Interactive activity — available in the OLI platform)*","position":{"start":{"line":76,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"key":"Z7m5u3SsZi"}],"key":"u0YFTEV1Rg"}],"key":"ElrCqlQU14"}],"key":"fgum09qXFX"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Two Independent Samples (1 of 7)","url":"/pages/m16-two-independent-samples-1-of-7","group":"Module 16: Inference for Relationships"},"next":{"title":"Two Independent Samples (3 of 7)","url":"/pages/m16-two-independent-samples-3-of-7","group":"Module 16: Inference for Relationships"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}